1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,120
How Wavefront works on Uniswap v4, deeply enough that you can open WavefrontHook.sol and follow it.

2
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:12,547
Every number was recorded from a real run of the contracts. Every snippet shows its file and line.

3
00:00:16,547 --> 00:00:30,087
v1 is one contract, Coin.sol. It stores no price, only reserves: real quote paid in, a virtual quote that sets the depth, coins in the pool, and the max supply.

4
00:00:30,087 --> 00:00:39,167
The price is virtual plus real quote, over the coins in the pool. A buy moves along a constant-product curve.

5
00:00:39,167 --> 00:00:47,063
The floor is virtual quote over max supply: roughly what the last seller gets once everyone else has sold.

6
00:00:47,863 --> 00:01:03,227
Each trade pays a 2% fee. A fifth goes to the team, a fifth to the treasury. On a buy, the rest is a heal: quote added to the reserves, which lifts the whole curve.

7
00:01:04,027 --> 00:01:13,387
On a sell, the rest is a burn: coins leave circulation, the pool gives up coins in the same proportion, and the floor rises again.

8
00:01:13,387 --> 00:01:22,467
Remember three numbers: the real quote, the coins in the pool, and the supply. v4 keeps exactly those.

9
00:01:26,467 --> 00:01:36,067
v4 keeps every pool inside one contract, the PoolManager. Routers, wallets and hooks all call into it.

10
00:01:36,067 --> 00:01:47,027
Nothing happens until a caller unlocks it. Inside, every swap and liquidity change is recorded as a delta: a tab of who owes what.

11
00:01:47,027 --> 00:01:57,967
Tokens go in with settle and out with take. When the unlock ends, every tab must be zero, or the whole transaction reverts.

12
00:01:57,967 --> 00:02:10,258
A credit can also be kept as an ERC-6909 claim, a balance held inside the PoolManager. The hook uses these a lot.

13
00:02:11,058 --> 00:02:22,778
Prices live on ticks, each a factor of 1.0001. With a tick spacing of 10, a position's edges sit on a grid about 0.1% apart.

14
00:02:22,778 --> 00:02:33,659
A position covers a range of ticks. Below the price it holds the quote token, above it the coin. A swap walks the price along it.

15
00:02:34,459 --> 00:02:49,239
A hook is called by the pool at fixed points. This one asks for three: two just revert, so only the hook can create these pools or add liquidity. The third, afterSwap, does all the work.

16
00:02:49,239 --> 00:02:57,875
One practical cost: Uniswap Labs' router only routes through afterSwap hooks once they are allowlisted by hand.

17
00:03:01,875 --> 00:03:11,035
Everything rests on this: v1's curve is exactly one concentrated-liquidity position, from the floor price to the top of v4's range.

18
00:03:11,035 --> 00:03:25,915
With liquidity L, s the square root of the price and a that of the floor: virtual quote is L·a, max supply L/a, real quote L·(s − a), and pool coins L/s.

19
00:03:25,915 --> 00:03:31,897
So the hook owns a single position, and any v4 router or scanner sees an ordinary pool.

20
00:03:32,697 --> 00:03:49,717
From three reserves, real quote X, pool coins y and circulating coins C, sqrtFloorFor gives v1's floor: √(X·y / (C·S)), where S = C + y.

21
00:03:49,717 --> 00:03:59,345
A heal grows X; a burn shrinks C and y together. Neither needs a formula of its own: change the reserves and the floor follows.

22
00:04:03,345 --> 00:04:15,894
The recorded coin, WAVE: one billion supply, 100,000 USDC of virtual quote, a 2% fee, and a 10,000 USDC buy by its creator at launch.

23
00:04:16,694 --> 00:04:25,514
Alice buys with 200 USDC. She calls the router, which unlocks the PoolManager and, in its callback, swaps.

24
00:04:25,514 --> 00:04:36,314
Plain v4 maths: the pool keeps 2%, 4.00 USDC, as the position's fee, and the rest buys 1,603,254 WAVE. Nothing has been paid yet.

25
00:04:36,314 --> 00:04:40,224
Then the PoolManager calls the hook's afterSwap.

26
00:04:41,024 --> 00:04:49,004
afterSwap first destroys coins an earlier rebalance left in the burn queue; here, only rounding dust.

27
00:04:49,004 --> 00:04:58,401
Then it touches its position with a liquidity change of zero, which makes v4 pay out the fees the position earned.

28
00:04:59,201 --> 00:05:09,301
_accountFees credits 20% each to the team and the treasury, to claim later. The other 60%, 2.40 USDC, becomes pending heal.

29
00:05:09,301 --> 00:05:17,832
It keeps the fee as claims, not tokens: Alice has not paid yet, so the USDC is not in the PoolManager to take.

30
00:05:18,632 --> 00:05:25,712
_ratchetDue rebuilds v1's three numbers, adds what is pending, and finds the floor they would support: tick −368,305.9.

31
00:05:25,712 --> 00:05:33,264
The floor is on −368,310 and moves only in steps of 10. Not yet, so nothing else happens.

32
00:05:34,064 --> 00:05:38,524
Back in the router: 200 USDC is pulled from Alice and settled.

33
00:05:38,524 --> 00:05:48,855
Her coins are taken out, every delta is zero, and the unlock closes. A buy like this costs about 80k gas.

34
00:05:52,855 --> 00:06:00,950
Bob buys with 3,000 USDC. The pool keeps 60, and pending heal grows to 38.40 USDC: this time, worth a step.

35
00:06:01,750 --> 00:06:12,590
_rebalance first removes the whole position: v4 cannot move a position's edge or depth in place. For a moment the pool holds no liquidity.

36
00:06:12,590 --> 00:06:20,850
_applyPending adds the heal to the reserves, as v1 would. The new floor rounds down to the grid: −368,280, three steps up.

37
00:06:20,850 --> 00:06:26,550
Down, because rounding up would promise the last sellers more than the pool holds.

38
00:06:26,550 --> 00:06:32,518
sqrtPriceFor then finds where v1's curve at that floor puts the price, given the coins in the pool.

39
00:06:33,318 --> 00:06:43,098
_addPosition moves the price there with a swap that trades nothing, since the pool is empty. Indexers see a zero-amount Swap.

40
00:06:43,098 --> 00:06:53,694
Then it adds the new, deeper position. It takes every coin back, plus 39.22 USDC more than came out: the heal entering the curve.

41
00:06:54,494 --> 00:07:03,434
Of the 40.10 USDC waiting, 0.88 stays idle, too little for another step. It is counted again next time.

42
00:07:03,434 --> 00:07:13,013
Floor and price both stepped up about 0.3%, inside Bob's swap. This trade cost about 357k gas.

43
00:07:17,013 --> 00:07:25,133
A sell mirrors a buy, except the fee is in coins, and the hook's share is burned rather than added.

44
00:07:25,133 --> 00:07:36,473
Alice sells 400,813 WAVE. Of the 8,016-coin fee, 1,603 each go to team and treasury, and 4,810 become pending burn.

45
00:07:36,473 --> 00:07:45,893
A burn lifts the floor by about its share of circulating coins; a step needs about 0.1%. This one waits.

46
00:07:46,693 --> 00:07:56,973
Bob sells all his coins. Pending burn reaches 285,476 WAVE, 0.314% of circulation: worth a step.

47
00:07:56,973 --> 00:08:04,033
_applyPending takes it out of circulation, and the pool gives up 2,854,564 coins in the same proportion.

48
00:08:04,033 --> 00:08:12,115
Fewer pool coins means a higher price; a smaller supply means a thinner curve. The floor rounds down to −368,250, three steps up.

49
00:08:12,915 --> 00:08:22,695
Those coins cannot be destroyed yet: Bob has not paid, so the PoolManager may not hold them. 3,140,041 WAVE wait in the burn queue.

50
00:08:22,695 --> 00:08:28,295
Queued coins stop counting as supply at once, so the curve already treats them as gone.

51
00:08:28,295 --> 00:08:37,802
The next swap's _flushBurnQueue redeems the claims and burns them. Supply falls from one billion to 996,859,959 WAVE.

52
00:08:41,802 --> 00:08:52,202
Alice holds 1,997,041 WAVE. Her credit is what they would fetch if sold last, at the bottom of the curve: 152.86 USDC. She borrows half.

53
00:08:52,202 --> 00:09:00,544
The quote in the lowest slice of the position can only be reached by selling the coins Alice now locks. Locked coins cannot be sold.

54
00:09:01,344 --> 00:09:08,604
borrow records the debt and rebalances. Floor and depth stay put; only the position's lower edge moves.

55
00:09:08,604 --> 00:09:18,404
The debt boundary, floor plus debt over liquidity, rounds up to the grid: two steps up. Starting there, the position needs 101.56 USDC less.

56
00:09:18,404 --> 00:09:27,830
Alice receives 76.43 USDC; the other 28.68 stays idle. Then borrow checks she still holds every coin that must be locked.

57
00:09:28,630 --> 00:09:35,950
The lock lives in the coin: every transfer asks the hook how many of the sender's coins are locked, and will not move them.

58
00:09:35,950 --> 00:09:45,670
Alice locks 1,248,225 WAVE: the coins backing her debt, plus a fixed margin of one grid step at the floor that covers the tick rounding.

59
00:09:45,670 --> 00:09:57,504
No interest and no liquidations. Repaying rebuilds the position from the floor. Borrow costs about 405k gas, repay about 313k.

60
00:10:01,504 --> 00:10:13,684
Unlike v1, the fee is chosen per coin, 1% to 5%, fixed for good, and the floor sits on a tick, at most 0.1% under v1's.

61
00:10:13,684 --> 00:10:23,814
Fees wait until they are worth a step, and team and treasury fees are claimed, not pushed, because inside a swap the trader has not paid yet.

62
00:10:23,814 --> 00:10:32,300
On 400-trade random paths the price was observed within 1% of v1's. The test itself requires 2%.

63
00:10:33,100 --> 00:10:41,840
Known limits: a sell into an empty range can cost millions of gas, and zero-amount Swap events can look like wicks on a chart.

64
00:10:41,840 --> 00:10:50,817
Waiting fees go to whoever first takes holders past 0.1%. The hook is 23,528 of 24,576 bytes, and it is unaudited.

65
00:10:51,617 --> 00:11:01,057
Read next: the CurveMath header, then _afterSwap, _ratchetDue, _rebalance, _applyPending, _addPosition, borrow and Coin._update.

66
00:11:01,057 --> 00:11:06,399
Then the tests: CurveMath.t.sol checks every formula against the real v1 contract.

